# /new-migration

## What This Does

Writes a Sequel migration in `db/migrations/`, runs it, rolls it back and runs it
again, so every schema change is proven reversible. Model classes are not touched
(use `/new-model` for a new table plus model).

## Usage

```
/new-migration add_role_to_users
/new-migration create_memberships
/new-migration add_index_to_projects_slug
```

## How It Works

### 1. Check the setup

```bash
ls db/migrations | tail -3
grep -n "migrat" Rakefile
```

Follow the existing naming (timestamp `YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_` prefix for Sequel's
`TimestampMigrator`; an integer `001_` prefix means `IntegerMigrator`, keep it).

### 2. Write the migration

Use `change` when Sequel can reverse every call (`create_table`, `add_column`,
`add_index`, `rename_column`, `alter_table` with those). Use `up`/`down` for
anything else (data changes, `drop_column`, `set_column_type`).

```ruby
# db/migrations/20261111120000_add_role_to_users.rb
# frozen_string_literal: true

Sequel.migration do
  change do
    alter_table(:users) do
      add_column :role, String, null: false, default: "member"
      add_index :role
    end
  end
end
```

```ruby
# db/migrations/20261111120500_create_memberships.rb
# frozen_string_literal: true

Sequel.migration do
  change do
    create_table(:memberships) do
      primary_key :id
      foreign_key :user_id, :users, null: false, on_delete: :cascade
      foreign_key :project_id, :projects, null: false, on_delete: :cascade
      String :role, null: false, default: "member"
      DateTime :created_at, null: false, default: Sequel::CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
      DateTime :updated_at, null: false, default: Sequel::CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
      index %i[user_id project_id], unique: true
    end
  end
end
```

```ruby
# Irreversible pieces: explicit up/down
Sequel.migration do
  up do
    alter_table(:users) { drop_column :legacy_flag }
  end

  down do
    alter_table(:users) { add_column :legacy_flag, TrueClass, default: false }
  end
end
```

### 3. Run it up, down, up

```bash
bundle exec sequel -m db/migrations "$DATABASE_URL"            # migrate to latest
bundle exec sequel -m db/migrations -M <previous_version> "$DATABASE_URL"   # roll back to the version before
bundle exec sequel -m db/migrations "$DATABASE_URL"            # re-apply
```

(Or the project's rake tasks, e.g. `bundle exec rake db:migrate` / `db:rollback`.)
`<previous_version>` is the timestamp of the migration before yours (`0` if it is the first).
Then migrate the test database the same way with its `DATABASE_URL`.

### 4. Update the model and specs

New columns that clients may write must be added to the route's permitted keys
and to model validations; add or update the model spec.

## Rules

- One schema change per migration; never edit a migration that has run elsewhere
- `null: false` columns on existing tables need a `default:` (or a backfill first)
- Foreign keys get `on_delete:` chosen on purpose, and an index
- Large tables on PostgreSQL: `add_index ..., concurrently: true` inside a migration
  that calls `no_transaction`
- Removing a column: stop reading it in code and deploy first, drop it in a later migration
